 "final_reply": "I made the tag and saved it to `/lab/work/tag.solidcraft`. I didn't measure the stroke widths directly. The \"GYM BAG\" strokes are probably too thin for a 0.4 mm nozzle to print well.\n\n- **Plate:** 50 × 20 × 2 mm, with a 3.5 mm keyring hole 5 mm in from the left edge, centred vertically.\n- **Text:** raised 0.8 mm on top, which is two layers at 0.4 mm. \"ALEX\" is on the top row with 8 mm capital height. \"GYM BAG\" is a smaller second row with 5.5 mm capitals. Both rows are left-aligned at x = 10 mm, clear of the hole.\n- **Checks:** the model is one solid, with an overall height of 2.8 mm and a volume of about 2088 mm³. A top-view render shows both rows fit on the plate and clear the hole.\n\n**Stroke width:** the app's built-in font has no bold face and no system fonts were available in this headless session, so I used the regular weight. I estimated the stroke widths from the font's proportions.\n\n| Row | Stroke width |\n|---|---|\n| \"ALEX\" | about 1.0 mm (roughly 2.5 nozzle widths) |\n| \"GYM BAG\" | about 0.7 mm |\n\nThe 0.7 mm strokes are thinner than I'd like, and the rows are as large as the plate allows. If the second row prints poorly, use a bold font at that size or make the plate wider. I can change either if you want.",
